meta { title = "Conditions and matching", description = "Use Boolean decisions, local loops and exhaustive matching.", order = 4, group = "Language reference" }

Bool has the values true and false. Comparisons return Bool. Use not, and and or for Boolean logic. and and or evaluate only the operands needed to determine the result.

h2 { "Select a result" }

codeblock { language = kalandra, """fn clampLow(value: int, minimum: int) -> int:
  if value < minimum:
    minimum
  else:
    value

fn main() -> int:
  clampLow(12, 42)
""" }

This returns 42. A general if expression needs both branches and a compatible result type. Only the selected branch executes, and bindings introduced in it stay within that branch. A non-final validation guard may omit else when its body ends in an explicit fail.

h2 { "Combine conditions" }

codeblock { language = kalandra, """fn main() -> int:
  value = 42
  valid = value >= 0 and value < 100
  if valid and not false:
    value
  else:
    0
""" }

This also returns 42. Write separate comparisons joined with and. Ungrouped chained comparisons are rejected. Floating-point comparisons follow the numerical rules, including NaN behavior; Boolean arrays and numerical tensors remain distinct types.

h2 { "Update local state in a loop" }

Numeric and Bool scalars can be reassigned without a mut declaration. Numeric array bindings can also be reassigned in CPU functions, including across task waits and expected-error handling. Their lengths may change; their element type stays fixed. A while statement reevaluates its Bool condition before each iteration. It may run zero times. Supply the function result separately after the loop; while does not produce an expression value.

codeblock { language = kalandra, """fn main() -> int:
  index = 0
  total = 0
  while index < 5:
    if index < 3:
      total = total + index
    else:
      total = total + 10
    index = index + 1
  total
""" }

This returns 23. A statement if may omit else; a value-producing if still needs both branches. Assignment to an existing scalar changes that binding; a new name introduced in a branch or iteration stays in that scope. Reassignment preserves the scalar type. Checked numeric/Bool array element writes are described in the array tutorial. break, continue and for loops are not supported.

Tasks started in an iteration must settle before the next iteration. A task started before the loop may still be running after it: an await inside the body might never execute if the loop runs zero times. Wait for that task outside the loop before writing storage it reads. Cancellation and resource limits apply to loops.

h2 { "Match a declared variant" }

Use match to select an enum or error alternative. Cover every alternative or use a final _ wildcard branch. Names bound to a variant's fields exist only in the selected branch. Match guards and nested destructuring are not supported.

